      "text": "Let $A' = AE \\cap BC$, and $B'E_1$ the tangent to $(A'E)$ at $B'$.\nBy Pythagoras, $$e_1^2:O{E}_{1}^{2}=OE^{2}$$\nHence, $CE:E'C=\\sqrt{O_{E}^{2}} :\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2}O_{E}^{2}-e_1^{2})}\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2} O_{E}^{2}+e_1^{2})}$\n\nLet $\\angle AEO=r^{\\circ}$, then $\\angle EOE_1=(90+r)^{\\circ}$. Thus, $\\angle BEA = (90+r)-(90-r)=2r^{\\circ}$, and so,\n$ \\angle EBO = \\angle BEA- \\angle EAB = 2r^{\\circ}-43^{\\circ}$. Since $C$ is the tangent to $\\odot(A'E)$ at $B'$, $CE:BE=o_o: \\sin\\angle COE_1=\\sin\\angle BEO$, which means that\n$\\frac{\\cos(90-r) \\sin(\\angle BEO+ r)}{\\sin(\\angle BEO+r)}=\\frac{1}{\\sin(\\angle BEO+r)}$ . This yields $\\sin{90-r} = \\cos{100-r}$. Then $\\cos{r} = \\cos{20}$  $\\implies$     \n$r=90^{\\circ}-20^{\\circ} = 70^{\\circ}$, so the solution is $\\angle CED=90 - 70 = 20^{\\circ} \\boxed{20}$",
